  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cooler airmass continues to build into the area\nbehind yesterday's cold front as high pressure expands in from \nthe west. Today will be the coolest day of the week until next \nweekend (see the next key message) with highs in the low to mid \n60s. With a second cold front moving through tonight, it will be\ndifficult for winds to remain calm, and as a result radiational\ncooling should be reduced. No widespread frost is expected\ntonight, although some patchy frost can't be ruled out in\nsheltered locations. \n\nMonday night continues to look like the best candidate for more\nwidespread frost as skies remain clear and winds inland will\nlikely be calm as the high crests overhead. Continued nudging \novernight lows down from NBM, closer to NBM 10th percentile. \nAdded patchy frost for some inland locales Monday night. Tuesday\nnight there remains a bit of uncertainty on how effectively we \nradiationally cool as the forecast calls for higher cloud cover\nthan Monday night. With that being said, decided to stick with \nNBM for now. However, if we end up being clear Tuesday night, \nanother round of frost is possible. \n\nBeyond this period, guidance continues to depict an \nincreasingly unsettled pattern as complex mid-level low deepens \nover the eastern CONUS and likely drives coastal low development\noff the east coast. Evolution and strength of the low remain\nuncertain, but it will at least bring our best chances of rain \nfor the period and breezier westerly winds across the Outer \nBanks. Stay tuned for more details in the coming days.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance continues to strongly hint at \nunseasonable cold temperatures next weekend behind the low \npressure system. While there is inherent uncertainty 7 days out,\nCPC does have a slight risk of much below normal temperatures \nfor our coastal plain counties next weekend. This will be worth \nmonitoring for any freeze headlines, as the end of the growing \nseason is Nov 25 for our land forecast zones.",
